('atlassian', 'Atlassian', 'dev', 'Search, create, and update Jira issues, Confluence pages, Jira Service Management requests, and Bitbucket content across your Atlassian Cloud.', '/services/atlassian.svg?tone=dark', '{"name":"Atlassian","slug":"atlassian","skills":[{"body":"# Atlassian\n\nUse the atlassian_* tools to interact with your Atlassian Cloud site (Jira, Confluence, Jira Service Management, Bitbucket) through the official hosted Rovo MCP server.\n\n## What you can do\n- Search and summarize Jira issues, Confluence pages, and JSM requests.\n- Create and update Jira work items and Confluence pages from natural-language instructions.\n- Look up Bitbucket content and repository information.\n\n## Auth\nYou either signed in through a browser OAuth 2.1 consent flow, or the connection uses an HTTP Basic Authorization header built from a personal API token (base64 of `email:api_token`, created at https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens). If tools return 401/403, re-authorize (OAuth) or check that the token is valid and not revoked.\n\n## Tips\n- Reference your Atlassian site/project by its key (e.g. Jira project key) when creating or querying issues.\n- Prefer searching before creating to avoid duplicate issues or pages.","name":"atlassian-usage","description":"How to use the atlassian_* tools to work with Jira, Confluence, Jira Service Management, and Bitbucket."}],"summary":"Search, create, and update Jira issues, Confluence pages, Jira Service Management requests, and Bitbucket content across your Atlassian Cloud.","category":"dev","authMethods":[{"id":"oauth","kind":"oauth","label":"Sign in with Atlassian","inputs":[],"envKeys":[],"mcpServers":[{"env":{},"url":"https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/mcp","args":[],"name":"atlassian","oauth":{},"headers":{},"transport":"http"}],"recommended":true},{"id":"api-token","kind":"key","label":"API token","inputs":[{"key":"ATLASSIAN_BASIC_AUTH","help":"1) Create an API token at https://id.atlassian.com/manage