('notion', 'Notion', 'productivity', 'Search, read and update your Notion pages and databases, comment on them and manage database views.', '/services/notion.svg?tone=dark', '{"name":"Notion","slug":"notion","skills":[{"body":"# Notion\n\nUse the notion tools to search, read and update pages and databases. Always confirm before creating or overwriting content.\n\n## What the connection mode changes\n\n**Sign in with Notion** (Notion''s hosted MCP server): the agent inherits the signed-in user''s own Notion permissions and gets the full toolset, including comments and database views. Views are created and edited with `create-view` / `update-view`: type `table`, `board`, `list`, `calendar`, `timeline`, `gallery`, `form`, `chart` or `map`, configured with a DSL (`FILTER \"Status\" = \"Done\"`, `SORT BY \"Created\" DESC`, `GROUP BY \"Priority\"`, `SHOW`, `CLEAR FILTER`). A board view requires a `GROUP BY`.\n\n**Internal integration token** (the `@notionhq/notion-mcp-server` package run locally): the agent only reaches the pages and databases added to the integration''s Content access tab, so if something is missing, ask the user to add it there. Two things are out of reach in this mode:\n\n- Creating a comment fails with `missing_version — Notion-Version header should be defined`. This is a bug in the package itself: `POST /v1/comments` is the only operation its bundled OpenAPI spec leaves without a `Notion-Version` header. Retrying, changing the payload or using another tool will not work around it.\n- Database views cannot be listed, created or edited at all. Notion''s public REST API has no view endpoints.\n\nIn both cases, do not retry: tell the user that this needs the \"Sign in with Notion\" connection mode, and that they can switch it from the connector panel.\n\nIf you ever call Notion''s REST API directly rather than through these tools, every single request needs a `Notion-Version` header, for example `Notion-Version: 2025-09-03`.","name":"notion-usage","description":"How to use Notion tools"}],"summary":"Search, read and update your Notion pages and databases, comment on them and manage database views.","category":"productivity","authMethods":[{"id":"oauth","kind":"oauth","label":"Sign in with Notion","inputs":[],"envKeys":[],"mcpServers":[{"env":{},"url":"https://mcp.notion.com/mcp","args":[],"name":"notion","oauth":{},"headers":{},"transport":"http"}],"recommended":true},{"id":"integration-token","kind":"stdio","label":"Internal integration token","inputs":[{"key":"NOTION_TOKEN","help":"Create an internal integration at notion.so/my-integrations and copy its Access Token. Then open the integration''s Content access tab and add the pages or databases the agent should be able to reach. This mode covers pages and databases only: it cannot manage database views, and creating comments currently fails because of a bug in Notion''s own MCP package. Pick Sign in with Notion for those.","type":"password","label":"Notion access token","secret":true,"required":true,"placeholder":"ntn_…"}],"envKeys":[],"mcpServers":[{"env":{"NOTION_TOKEN":"${input.NOTION_TOKEN}"},"args":["-y","@notionhq/notion-mcp-server"],"name":"notion","command":"npx","headers":{},"transport":"stdio"}],"recommended":false}],"description":"Connects Notion so the agent can search, read and update pages and databases, comment on them and manage database views. Sign in through Notion''s hosted MCP with browser OAuth for the full toolset, including comments and board, table, calendar, timeline, gallery, chart and map views with filters, sorts and grouping. Or run the official MCP server locally with an internal integration token, which covers pages and databases only.","multiInstance":true}'::jsonb, true, true, '[-0.009597778,0.04928589,0.0501709,-0.004886627,-0.022491455,-0.011421204,0.024658203,0.076538086,-0.012634277,-0.010177612,-0.016311646,0.019332886,-0.014678955,-0.0066719055,-0.003818512,0.017822266,-0.022354126,-0.037261963,0.00187397,0.06768799,0.055908